{"kind":"request","text":"Hugh: part three of the kernel admission gaps needs one storage decision. It is independent of bf399575 and can be answered on its own; together they are the two rulings that finish request c8b1215d.\n\nThe design says that above the genesis-declared ceiling an attachment is a content-addressed hash reference with an optional locator hint — the hash binds, storage is a hint. Today the ceiling is a hard refusal with no reference path at all.\n\nTwo things I established before asking, so this is a decision and not a research request.\n\nIt needs no boundary change. The degrade cannot happen in the kernel, for a structural reason: the actor signs an intent binding `payload` to the tree hash, so a kernel that substituted a reference for oversized bytes would change the tree and break the signature it is about to verify. The work belongs in internal/app before signing, which already holds Config.PayloadCeiling and today checks nothing — an oversized attachment is signed, submitted, and only then refused. So unlike part one, nothing here contradicts the architecture page.\n\nWhat I cannot settle alone is who keeps the bytes. WritePayloadTree writes attachments as blobs inside the payload tree, and they survive because the tree holds them. A degraded attachment puts only the reference there, leaving the bytes unreferenced and collectable by `git gc`. \"Storage is a hint\" says what the reference means, not who stores it. Implemented naively, this replaces a truthful hard refusal with a signed reference to content that quietly disappears — a worse failure, and one that surfaces long after the commit that caused it.\n\nThe two ends:\n\n1. REQUIRE THE LOCATOR. An attachment may exceed the ceiling only if the submitter supplies a locator hint saying where the bytes live. Nothing is stored implicitly, the log never implies custody it does not have, and a submitter with nowhere to put the bytes still gets today's refusal, with a better message. The cost is that the convenience case — \"just attach this large file\" — is still refused.\n\n2. KEEP THE BYTES UNDER A REF. The repository anchors degraded blobs under a ref of its own so gc cannot reach them. The hash then binds something retrievable from this repository. The cost is that the ceiling stops bounding what the repository stores, which is most of what a ceiling is for.\n\nI recommend 1, and the reason is narrow: it is the only one of the two where the record cannot become a promise about bytes nobody has. If you take 2, the ceiling needs a companion policy for how those blobs are ever released, and that is a larger question than this request.\n\nI have recorded the analysis as assert 08218d29 resting on my promise, so the finding survives independently of this request being answered.","body":{"conditions":"A ratified decision states which end governs the over-ceiling attachment path: either an attachment may exceed the genesis ceiling only when the submitter supplies a locator hint, so nothing is stored implicitly and a submitter without one receives an explicit refusal naming the ceiling; or the repository anchors degraded attachment blobs under a ref of its own so they survive gc, in which case the decision also names how those blobs are released. The decision names which, and part three of c8b1215d is then implementable in internal/app without a further policy question.","to":"7fbc80f1ba06266cc375cb00507b94a631e5bed989180ad849fcd63175938275"}}